 very very briskly paced it's very  action-packed and it kind of just like  sucks you right in from the very  beginning with like this whole mystery  of like what exactly is like going on  here and then like as it goes on it just  keeps steadily like ramping up the  stakes and ramping up the violence um  until you get to the ending which is  kind of like pretty balls to the wall  it's also i have to say one of very few  horror novels that i've read  where  there really aren't any  all good guys or all bad guys and you  know i i know that's not that rare but  i'm just saying that  in the sense that everybody in this um  well most people in this like have done  kind of terrible things but  i think like the magic of the book is  that the reader can sympathize with  everyone's perspective because everyone  is just very  nuanced and very human and it's like  they do terrible things but you can kind  of like understand how they got into  that position and things like that so  to me like the moral ambiguity i guess  of the story was a real highlight um  because it's sort of like mirrored  reality to quite an uncomfortable extent  but i really really did like  kind of where it was coming from with  that so at the start of the novel you  get this kind of brief prologue in which  someone is talking about they seem to be  like in some kind of limbo state like  unaware of who they are or where they  are they can't seem to see or hear  anything they can't like feel their body  or like tell if they're moving or even  tell if they're inside a body and their  consciousness just kind of seems to be  floating out there in the ether  so then we delve into the story proper  now most of the book is told from the  point of view of like a middle-aged guy  named joe gerhard and he seems like a  very amiable regular joe type of guy  now he manages a very secret secret  secret government facility that is kind  of hidden in the hills of i think it's  west virginia somewhere  and a lot of the 