 into the story though joe gets the  very shocking news that his ex-wife kate  has committed suicide which seems like  something that she wouldn't do so it's  like very confusing for him now riley  who at this point is 16 or 17 years old  uh she comes to live with him and he  hopes that he'll be able to kind of like  bridge the distance that's developed  between them over the years since they  got divorced and you find out later on  that the divorce was actually kind of  his fault because like as some [ __ ] up  [ __ ] that he did  however shortly after riley moves in  she starts seeing things uh specifically  she starts seeing this terrifying  looking woman with  deathly pale skin and greasy black hair  and she's wearing what appears to be a  hospital gown  now  alarmingly no one else seems to be able  to see her and at first  you know when she tells her dad about it  and tells hannah about it and stuff like  that they kind of think well you know  the kid's mom just  died very suddenly in a very horrible  traumatic way so obviously this is like  some grief processing because this woman  that she's seeing  kind of looks she knows that it's not  her mom but it kind of looks enough like  her mom that people are like thinking  that as the story goes on though like  everybody just thinks oh you know she'll  work through it because she's just  having problems because of grief or  whatever but as the story goes on and  strange things start happening at joe's  workplace as well that seem to be  connected with riley's sightings of this  woman whoever she is uh it starts to  become clear that something profound and  probably very very dangerous uh might be  happening within the minds of the  antennas that are being kept at this  facility so as i mentioned this was  actually a great read i mean it just  kind of like moved right along like  breakneck speed and it gets more and  more messed up like as it goes along now  the characters most of which worked at  the facility doing what are obviously  horrible things to helpless human beings  uh were still sympathetic because  they just came across as just kind of  like regular working people with their  own problems uh that just had a job to  do and tried not to think too hard about  like the bigger implications or  consequences of the work that they were  doing so you could kind of sympathize  with them to the extent that it's like  you could put yourself in  in their shoes um even though you  obviously don't you know want to think  that it's like well i would never like  do a job like that but it's just kind of  like but they were still like good  people you know what i'm saying um and  on the other hand you also felt really  bad for the antennas obviously because  they were  as i said helpless human beings you  don't really know where they came from  they could have just been snatched up  off the street for all you know or they  could have been like political prisoners  who knows where they came from and then  they're doing these horrible things like  they shave their heads and like they're  just laying there pooping themselves and  [ __ ] like that and they're just like  inflicting horrible pain on them and so  they're doing that  so you can kind of like  understand their anguish and their  motivations as well so the story was  actually really kind of nuanced in that  way it didn't really let any of the  characters off the hook for the stuff  that they did but  it also made you as the reader kind of  see how they got into the position of  doing those things and you could see how  that could happen you know what i mean  um so  overall like coming at it from that  angle it was kind of like a real  compelling take on how easy it is for  evil outcomes to maybe like emerge from  confused bureaucracies even if no one  within those bureaucracies is a  particularly bad person so it's kind of  that sort of thing you know what i mean  so if you're at all into kind of like  government conspiracy type stuff or  stuff that could maybe like marginally  be termed science fiction about you know  secret agencies doing human  experimentation like especially like in  regards to psychic abilities you know  stuff like my mention minority report or  maybe something like fire starter or  scanners or altered states or the fury  or something like that where there's  like some kind of faceless corporation  that's trying to weaponize psychic  powers essentially uh then you'll  probably really dig this because this is  right along those lines uh but keep in  mind that the sci-fi element while it is  a significant part of the story is kind  of more in service to the horror like i  said this is a horror story it's not  really a science fiction story you know  what i mean it has science fiction  elements but it's mostly a horror story  and uh believe me when i say that this  thing does get skin peelingly gory and i  actually do mean that quite literally so  if you're squeamish uh might want to  give it a pass there's like a lot of  people being torn apart and like stuff  like that so if you don't like that kind  of thing then maybe don't because  there's a lot of that in here but you  know what i mean uh but everybody else  should definitely check it out uh i  thought it was outstanding and it really  kind of like kept me engaged from the  first page and also had like a really  good like kind of gut punch of an ending  i think i don't know like i don't want  to say i necessarily saw the ending  coming but i was just kind of like it  almost had like this real like i don't  want to say depressing but like a real  like [ __ ] like inevitability to it  you're just like yeah of course you know  what i mean it was just that kind of  ending but yeah i mean if you have  kindle unlimited then i would definitely  recommend it it was real good i had a  good time with it and that will do it  for this tomes of terror and i will see  you guys again on the next one  bye

